        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "baa00d13-a15d-50f2-b648-41e197f054fe",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Leo Varadkar, Enda Kenny, Michael Noonan, Brendan Howlin\n- Roles: Leader of Fine Gael, Minister for Social Protection, Minister for Health, Minister for Transport Tourism and Sport, Minister for Finance, Leader of the Opposition, Minister for Industry and Commerce, Minister for Justice, Minister for Public Expenditure and Reform\n- Events: Irish budget 2012\n- Organizations: Fine Gael, 31st Dáil\n\nFacts:\n- Michael Noonan served as Minister for Justice from 1982 to 1986.\n- Michael Noonan served as Minister for Industry and Commerce from 1986 to 1987.\n- Michael Noonan served as Minister for Health from 1994 to 1997.\n- Michael Noonan served as Leader of Fine Gael and Leader of the Opposition from 2001 to 2002.\n- Leo Varadkar served as Minister for Transport, Tourism and Sport from 2011 to 2014.\n- Michael Noonan served as Minister for Finance from 2011 to 2017.\n- The Irish budget 2012 was the first budget of the 31st Dáil, with Brendan Howlin delivering the first part and Michael Noonan delivering the second part.\n- Leo Varadkar served as Minister for Health from 2014 to 2016.\n- Leo Varadkar served as Minister for Social Protection from 2016 to 2017.\n- Leo Varadkar became Leader of Fine Gael on 2 June 2017 following the resignation of Enda Kenny.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "25ba3c13-8ba6-556a-bf45-e95e84960ec5",
        "text": "Page: Leader of Fine Gael. Sentence: Since 2 June 2017, the office had been held by Leo Varadkar following the resignation of Enda Kenny.\nPage: Leo Varadkar. Sentence: He previously served as Minister for Social Protection from 2016 to 2017, Minister for Health from 2014 to 2016 and Minister for Transport, Tourism and Sport from 2011 to 2014.\nPage: Michael Noonan. Sentence: Michael James Noonan (born 21 May 1943) is an Irish Fine Gael politician who served as Minister for Finance from 2011 to 2017, Leader of the Opposition and Leader of Fine Gael from 2001 to 2002, Minister for Health from 1994 to 1997, Minister for Industry and Commerce from 1986 to 1987 and Minister for Justice from 1982 to 1986.\nPage: Irish budget, 2012. Sentence: It was the first budget of the 31st Dáil, with the first part delivered by Minister for Public Expenditure and Reform Brendan Howlin, and the second part delivered by Minister for Finance Michael Noonan.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
      },
